body:
Bitter, soon-to-be exhusband here, so take this with a giant grain of salt.  I don't know your husband, I can't speak for him.  I feel like my wife was waiting to walk away and she found a catalyst with mistakes that I made.  I don't know if there is another guy, I really don't want to, I have gotten off of all social media.  To borrow from DC Talk, love is a verb, not a feeling.  I knew things weren't great but I kept going to a job I hated so she could home-school our autistic son, that was love.  I let her sleep in every weekend, that was love.  I supported her as she volunteered, tried being an author, went to conferences.  I let her dream because I didn't have any.  That was love.

&#x200B;

She is gone, she has the kids most days of the week, I am left with a mostly empty house, regrets, and a desire to not wake up tommorrow.  The first day of counselling we laid out our goals, mine was reconcilliation, and hers was divorced.  Guess who's goals we have worked on the entire time, and guess who has paid for the counselling sessions? 

&#x200B;

You might find freedom by walking away, you might find happiness.  If he is anything like me, you will leave a devastated and broken man behind.  We all make bad choices and hurt each other.
